==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|brembel|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|bræmbel|lang=ang}}, from earlier {{term|brǣmel|lang=ang}}, {{term|brēmel|lang=ang}}, from dialectal {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*brēmila-}} (compare West Frisian {{term|brommel|lang=fy}}, Flemish {{term|bramel|lang=vls}}, Low German {{term|Brommelbeer|lang=nds}}), diminutive of {{m|gem-pro|*brēm-}} (compare English {{term|broom|lang=en}}). More at {{l|en|broom}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-bramble.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{IPA|/ˈbɹæmbəl/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|æmbəl|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|British}} Common [[blackberry]].
#*{{quote-book|year=1944|author={{w|Cecil Street|Miles Burton}}
|title=[https://openlibrary.org/works/OL10563347W The Three Corpse Trick]|chapter=5
|passage=The hovel stood in the centre of what had once been a vegetable garden, but was now a patch of rank weeds. Surrounding this, almost like a zareba, was an irregular ring of gorse and '''brambles''', an unclaimed vestige of the original common.}}
# {{label|en|US}} Any of several closely related thorny plants in the genus ''[[Rubus]]'', including [[blackberry]] and [[raspberry]].
# Any [[thorny]] [[shrub]].
# A [[cocktail]] of [[gin]], [[lemon juice]], and [[blackberry]] [[liqueur]].

